  <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

  <article class="book-prose">
    <h1 class="chapter-title">About Jeremy Fritzhand</h1>

    <p class="chapter-lead">
      <strong>Jeremy Fritzhand</strong> is an American textile entrepreneur, educator, and founder
      of Studio Bagru and Fritz MFG, recognized for documenting and advancing traditional hand block
      printing and ethical artisan craft.
    </p>

    <p>
      My work with hand block printing began in 2010, when I traveled to{' '}
      <strong>Bagru, Rajasthan</strong> as a <strong>Minerva Fellow</strong> from Union College.
      What began as a fellowship grant and a one-way ticket became years of living and working
      directly alongside master <em>Chhipa</em> printers, woodblock carvers, and natural dyers
      thirty kilometers southwest of Jaipur.
    </p>

    <h2>Studio Bagru, Mahila Print &amp; Fritz MFG</h2>
    <p>
      After launching early grassroots digital efforts under Bagru Textiles, I founded{' '}
      <strong>Studio Bagru</strong> in 2016—a transparent hand block printing and cut-and-sew
      manufacturer bridging rural Rajasthan artisans directly with independent international brands
      including Story MFG, LRNCE, and Molly Mahon Textiles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Within that network, we created <strong>Mahila Print</strong> to tackle creative equity
      directly: recognizing women artisans and block printers as original designers entitled to
      direct financial compensation and credit for their design rights, rather than flat piece-rate
      labor alone. As local printing families built direct global relationships, Studio Bagru
      evolved into an open directory and buyer’s guide indexing authentic Bagru workshops without a
      middleman.
    </p>
    <p>
      In 2023, I founded <strong>Fritz MFG</strong> for sustainable textile sourcing, creative
      consultancy, and hands-on educational block-printing workshops—alongside my startup ecosystem
      leadership as former CEO of VentureStudio at Ahmedabad University, Director of Startup
      Services at Alloy Growth Lab, and VP of Platform &amp; Member Engagement at Embarc Collective.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this online book exists</h2>
    <p>
      Most online writing about hand block printing is written by marketers who have never stood at
      a padded <em>patia</em> table or mixed a <em>dabu</em> clay vat.{' '}
      <strong>Blockprinting.org</strong> is an ad-free, permanently updated technical reference
      capturing the real timber, chemistry, regional lineages, and workshop economics of the craft.
    </p>

